[[ <shot prompt="Wide shot of a well-worn farmhouse front porch in blue twilight: three men along the rail with sweating glasses of iced tea; at left a big bearded man in his late forties with thick black hair; at center the largest of them, a broad barrel-chested gentle giant of thirty-nine with sandy hair and a short beard in a red plaid shirt; at right a slim fine-boned man of thirty-nine with dark hair swept back, clean-shaven, in a maroon sweater; a glowing blue mosquito zapper at the far end of the porch; warm kitchen light spilling from the windows behind them. The bearded man at left is the man from the first reference image, fully clothed in a rolled-sleeve work shirt and jeans. The gentle giant at center is the man from the second reference image, in a red plaid shirt. The slim man at right is the man from the third reference image, in a maroon sweater. Exactly three people in the image. Each person has exactly two arms and two hands; each hand has five fingers with natural proportions. No extra fingers, no extra hands, no extra arms. No text or lettering anywhere in the image." ref="HUNTER,GARETH,KEVIN" output="storyboards/ep1_sc1_08_three-men.png"/> ]]

NARRATOR
The men don't so much hug, as lean against each other, and exchange slaps on the back. Gareth hands out the glasses of iced tea, and everyone sits.

NARRATOR
On the opposite end of the porch, the glowing blue mosquito zapper claims another victim with a buzz.

KEVIN
(droll)
Okay, so. The house looks the same, Mom's cooking too much, Dad already drew you a map, and you've been home ninety seconds. I'd say we're right on schedule.

HUNTER
He offered a napkin.

KEVIN
(droll)
He always offers the napkin. The napkin is love. You want to see a man cry, accept the napkin. How long has it been since you've been back?

HUNTER
Too long. When dad was sick.

KEVIN
And you left early.

HUNTER
Kevin, I know you were disappointed I couldn't--

KEVIN
Look, don't. Ok. That's water under the bridge. We've already--

HUNTER
No, it wasn't fair, that the responsibility of mom and dad, all fell on you. I'm here to help. I want to do what I can to help.

[[ <shot prompt="Wide shot of a well-worn farmhouse front porch in blue twilight, three men only: at left, standing at the porch rail with his back half turned and his jaw tight, gripping the rail, a slim clean-shaven man of thirty-nine with dark hair swept back, in a maroon sweater; at center, seated in a porch chair, a very heavy man of thirty-nine with short untidy sandy light-brown hair and a short sandy beard, in a red plaid shirt, one big hand raised palm-out in a quiet stay gesture; at right, seated and frozen in his chair as if slapped, a man in his late forties with thick jet-black hair and a dense black beard, in a plain grey rolled-sleeve work shirt. A glowing blue mosquito zapper at the far end of the porch, warm kitchen light from the windows behind them. The man at left is the man from the first reference image. The man at right is the man from the second reference image, fully clothed, no plaid. The man at center is the man from the third reference image. Exactly three people in the image, one of each. Each person has exactly two arms and two hands; each hand has five fingers with natural proportions. No extra fingers, no extra hands, no extra arms. No text or lettering anywhere in the image." seed="7" ref="KEVIN,HUNTER,GARETH" output="storyboards/ep1_sc1_09_i-dont-believe-you.png"/> ]]

NARRATOR
Kevin stops talking and seems to draw into himself. When he speaks again, it’s with gritted teeth and almost a whisper.

KEVIN
I don’t fucking believe you.

NARRATOR
Hunter reacts like he’s been slapped.

HUNTER
What did I say?

NARRATOR
Gareth knows what's coming and can’t watch this. He's heard this before. Many times. Kevin stands and walks to the other side of the porch.

KEVIN
No, I can’t—

NARRATOR
Hunter starts to rise, but gets a “stay” hand motion from Gareth. He emphasizes it again watching Kevin pace.

KEVIN
I can’t believe you.

HUNTER
If you’d just tell me what—

KEVIN
(barely above a whisper)
For two years I’ve dealt with one thing after another with them. Dad’s colon cancer, Mom’s arthritis. Both of their blood sugar.

KEVIN
I’ve taken them to appointment after appointment, and I’ve sat by their beds, and chemo chairs, whenever it was needed, and now you think you can waltz back in here, and be the favorite son—

HUNTER
I was never—

KEVIN
You. Have. ALWAYS. Been the favorite. You’re the favorite, and I’m the pain in the ass.

From inside the house mom yells:

JOANN
Kevin, please. Language!

KEVIN
(whispering)
See! You have always been their favorite.

HUNTER
Well, maybe if you weren't such a potty mouth--

KEVIN
I’m the one that's always wrong. I'm the one that needs to be "managed."

