KEVIN
So I have to take him two towns over, to River City, to an appointment. Every two weeks, dad can fart.

[[ <shot prompt="Medium close shot on a farmhouse porch at night, three men only, side by side under a single warm porch bulb with moths circling: at left, a man in his late forties with thick jet-black hair and a dense black beard, in a plain grey rolled-sleeve work shirt, huffing a laugh that is already fading into something sadder, an iced tea glass forgotten in his hand; at center, a slim clean-shaven man of thirty-nine with dark hair swept back, in a maroon sweater, with a dry resigned half-smile; at right, a very heavy man of thirty-nine with short untidy sandy light-brown hair and a short sandy beard, in a red plaid shirt, chuckling softly. Deep blue night beyond the porch rail. The man at left is the man from the first reference image, fully clothed, no plaid. The man at center is the man from the second reference image. The man at right is the man from the third reference image. Exactly three people in the image, one of each. Each person has exactly two arms and two hands; each hand has five fingers with natural proportions. No extra fingers, no extra hands, no extra arms. No text or lettering anywhere in the image." seed="7" ref="HUNTER,KEVIN,GARETH" output="storyboards/ep1_sc1_12_fart-appointment.png"/> ]]

NARRATOR
Hunter huffs a laugh. Then it stops being funny. This is what Kevin has been doing every day he was gone.

HUNTER
I'll take him to the doctor's appointment, Kevin. Whatever it's for. But why is it all the way over in River City?

KEVIN
Dad doesn't want to take the chance that it gets back to any of his old codger friends, that he's having trouble farting.

KEVIN
Because he'll never hear the end of it.

NARRATOR
Hunter rolls his eyes.

