[[ <shot prompt="Wide shot at night from the porch toward the gravel drive: two brothers hauling suitcases and duffels out of the back of a dusty SUV and its U-Haul trailer, the big bearded man in his late forties carrying two bags, the slim dark-haired man of thirty-nine dragging a roller case over the gravel, both mid-argument; the farmhouse porch light throwing long warm shadows down the drive, stars coming out over the dark fields. The big bearded man is the man from the first reference image, fully clothed in a rolled-sleeve work shirt and jeans. The slim dark-haired man is the man from the second reference image, in a maroon sweater. The farmhouse, porch and gravel drive are the same house shown in the third reference image. Exactly two people in the image. Each person has exactly two arms and two hands; each hand has five fingers with natural proportions. No extra fingers, no extra hands, no extra arms. No text or lettering anywhere in the image." ref="HUNTER,KEVIN,house" output="storyboards/ep1_sc1_14_suitcases.png"/> ]]

NARRATOR
We follow two brothers back to the car. They grab suitcases and haul them back to the house, working through the running list of arguments they keep on file.

INT. RHODES HOUSE — KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

KEVIN
You're carrying that wrong.

NARRATOR
Indexed by subject matter, theirs is a steady rhythmic bicker. It is never, even once, about what it is really about.

HUNTER
I'm carrying it fine.

KEVIN
You're carrying it like it owes you money.

HUNTER
I don't know what that means. I don't speak drag queen, Roxy.

KEVIN
Your back's been shot since you were nineteen. Give me the dinosaur one, before I make you extinct.

HUNTER
I've got the dinosaur.

KEVIN
A dinosaur carrying a dinosaur. Never mind, keep it. It's completely on brand.

HUNTER
(yelling)
MOM! Kevin's bothering me!

JOANN
(passing the door on her way downstairs, not even slowing)
Stop bothering your brother, Kevin. Let him at least get in the door.

NARRATOR
Hunter shakes his head. 

HUNTER
I can't believe that still works.

KEVIN
(whisper)
I. Hate. You.

NARRATOR
They drop the bags in Hunter's childhood bedroom — where the boy will sleep, and where, for these first few nights at least, so will Hunter. The brothers turn to leave the room, but Kevin embraces him, suddenly.

KEVIN
(quietly)
...It's good you're back. I mean it.

HUNTER
(quietly)
Thanks.

INT. Rhodes House - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR
Downstairs. Toby sets the table for six. He's remembering a Christmas when they had to drag in an extra chair. Tonight his mother isn't here.

NARRATOR
He's still standing there when Grandma sets down a steaming bowl of mashed potatoes with three pats of butter melting on top. Grandma's mashed potatoes can break any melancholy.

Toby sits and the family joins him.

RAY
(over the noise, to Toby)
Pass your old grandpa the potatoes before Uncle Kevin takes the whole bowl.

KEVIN
(joking)
Settle down, old man. Nobody's takin' your potatoes.

JOANN
Honestly, Kevin. Leave some for everyone else.

NARRATOR
Everyone settles into eating silently. Then, because someone always does—

JOANN
(carefully)
And how's Holly doing, honey?

HUNTER
(smooth, flat)
She's dealing with some health issues. She's fine. Toby, you want more of the—

KEVIN
(a question)
"Health issues"?

HUNTER
Yes, health issues.

KEVIN
Is it a vagina problem?

Dad sets down his fork, forcefully, suddenly not as hungry.

KEVIN
It might be a vagina problem.

JOANN
Kevin, honestly. At the table?

TOBY
Dad, what's a vagina?

HUNTER
It's not a female reproductive problem.

KEVIN
I'm not... I'm just asking. It's me. It's us. We're your family. You can say the actual words at the actual table. I've spent the last two years, knee-deep in health problems, with people I love. I can take--

JOANN
(bright, steering)
Let's not get into all that at supper. Toby, sweetheart, there is a pie with your name on—

HUNTER
(gently)
Mom. It's okay. I want him to hear this. Because-

Hunter has one hand covering Toby's and one covering his mother's.

JOANN
Hunter—

HUNTER
Mom, it's okay. Really.

This part is for the family: the grown-ups. Toby already knows it and eats in silence.

HUNTER
There's nothing shameful here. Nobody's done anything to apologize for, and nobody should be thought of as weak. Holly is sick. That's the plain truth, she's sick. And she is doing the hard, brave work of getting better.

TOBY
She's doing...
(to his dad)
What's that word?

HUNTER
Introspection.

TOBY
"INSPECTION"

The mispronunciation makes Kevin and Joann smile. Dad doesn't know what to think.

HUNTER
Which, given her history, is actually one of the strongest moves she could make. And this family is not going to whisper about that, or pretend it isn't happening.

RAY
(gruff)
Nobody's whispering. Which might actually be the problem.

HUNTER
*I whispered*. For a long time I couldn't say any of this. But I'm not going to live that way and I'm not going to teach it to Toby.

HUNTER
So here's the rule now. The only way we get through the hard stuff is we say what we're feeling out loud. Even when it's scary. Especially when it's scary. Isn't that right, Tobes?

TOBY
(agreeing)
We tell the truth. Even about the sad stuff.

HUNTER
Even about the sad stuff.

HUNTER
(to the table)
So here it is, straight. Holly is battling depression. There were some days when she couldn't get off the couch. So we started seeing a counselor.

HUNTER
And when we sat in that room, what came up wasn't anybody being a villain. It was the way she and I had wired ourselves together — leaning so hard on each other there was no healthy way to stay married.

HUNTER
When it ended, she's the one who said, "Take Toby." Not because she doesn't love you. The opposite.

HUNTER
She knew she couldn't fight her way well and hold a job and raise you all at once, and something had to be set down so the rest could be carried.

TOBY
(quiet)
So you carried me.

HUNTER
All the way home. To Granville. And I took the job at the planning office, and here we are. Your mom's back in Minneapolis doing the work.

HUNTER
And her doctors set some real markers — real signs she's getting stronger. And one of them, buddy, one of the big ones, is her making the trip down here. On her own. To see you. That's going to happen soon.

TOBY
(not asking — telling the table, because he already knows)
She's coming to see me. Soon.

HUNTER
Soon. I promise.

KEVIN
(thin, letting it go)
Well. Won't that be... nice.

JOANN
And you, Gareth? All those miles? Weeks alone in that cab, it's not right, a person shouldn't—

HUNTER
(quiet, to Kevin)
What I want to know is what about you, Kev?

KEVIN
What about me?

HUNTER
Gareth’s trip seems like the perfect time for Roxy to go "On Tour".

Everyone reacts--little surprise, but mom smiles and that gives Hunter the courage to go all-in.

HUNTER
Sure. Do it. Go on the road. Ride along. Weeks in a cab beats weeks painting a bathroom alone. Book some gay clubs in the square states. They still have those, right?

KEVIN
(too fast)
No. No, I can't just— somebody's got to drive Dad to his—

RAY
(waving it off, head of the table)
I'm fine. I'm fine.

KEVIN
—to his appointments, which there are more of lately, whatever Dad says, and the house, and— no. I can't just leave.

HUNTER
Kev, I'm right here now, I can—

KEVIN
(the valve opening on the wrong thing)
Seriously, Hunter. You are not prepared for this.

KEVIN
I have seen things. Things I can never unsee.

HUNTER
Let me take a shift. Seriously. Tag me in. The appointments, the house, the heavy stuff — I'm here, all Dad has to do is ask.

RAY
Ah, your mother and I don't want to be a bother to you boys. We manage. We always have.

KEVIN
(not a word — then, flat)
...Whenever they ask. Right.

KEVIN
Based on what you know about this man, do you think he's ever going to ask anyone for help? He's worse than you are.

HUNTER
(serious)
Dad. When I was a kid, you never once waited to be asked. Every single time I needed something, help just showed up. No invoice. No bother. No score.

HUNTER
So this isn't me doing anybody a favor, and it's not charity. This is the part where I get to pay it back. It's my turn. And I'm not asking permission to take it.

KEVIN
...You mean that?

HUNTER
I mean it. Go.

KEVIN
(a breath, then)
...Okay. Okay. Yeah. I'll go.

GARETH
(delighted)
...I'll pack the big cooler.

HUNTER
(confidently)
And I already did one better than looking. I closed on a place last week. Ours. Our own front door, right here in Granville.

JOANN
(lighting up)
You bought a house? Hunter Rhodes, you bought a — where? Where is it?

HUNTER
(suddenly very interested in his plate)
It's — nearby. It's close. It's a fixer-upper, needs some work, but it's got good bones and a real yard and—

KEVIN
(catching the dodge, delighted)
He's not saying where.

RAY
(gruff, factual)
What do you mean he's not saying where. It's a house. It has an address. Houses have addresses.

HUNTER
It has an address.

RAY
So?

KEVIN
(gleeful)
Oh, this is good. Look at his face. He's blushing. Where's your house, Hunter?

HUNTER
(cracking, flat, to the potatoes)
...It's on the corner of Ball and Sack.

KEVIN
(losing it completely)
It's WHERE—

JOANN
Language—

KEVIN
I didn't say a — that's the STREETS, Ma, that's the literal — Ball and Sack! He bought a house on the corner of Ball and Sack!

RAY
(defending the corner on principle)
...Ezra Ball's people and the old Sack farm. Those roads've been named a hundred years. It is a perfectly good corner.

KEVIN
It is the best house in the United States of America, and I have never been prouder of my big brother's ball-sack house.

NARRATOR
Hunter's palm instinctively goes to his face, with a heavy sigh.

JOANN
We tell the truth. Even about the hard stuff.

KEVIN
If the ball-sack is the hard part, Mom, I think you might be doing it wrong.

NARRATOR
This makes Gareth spit iced tea out of his nose.

RAY
KEVIN!

JOANN^
KEVIN!

HUNTER
(smiling at Gareth)
Great! Kevin's got a built-in audience now. You know, Kev, they don't give out Oscars for being overly-dramatic in real life.

KEVIN
You audition for the role you want. Not the one you have.

HUNTER
(resignation)
I guess, if you live in the Ball-Sack house, you just gotta own it.

NARRATOR
The truth, said out loud at a supper table. In this family that's about twice as hard to swallow as the pie. They'll fold it up with the leftovers and carry it out to the porch.

.EXT. RHODES HOUSE — PORCH — LATER

RAY
(lobbying to the last)
Now you take the bypass out to the interstate, don't let him take the county road—

JOANN
(pressing foil)
There's the rest of the chicken, and the pie, and do not argue with me—

KEVIN
(over his shoulder)
Love you too, Ma.

NARRATOR
The guests go, and the house goes quiet.

NARRATOR
Upstairs, Toby is going to sleep in the room his father grew up in.

.INT. Rhodes House - BEDROOM — NIGHT

HUNTER
(murmured)
Okay, bud. New ceiling. I know. But that's your dinosaur guy right there in the corner, see him? He's on watch. Nobody gets past him.

TOBY
(more than half asleep)
...'s this home now?

HUNTER
(a beat too long)
This is home now. I've got you. Go to sleep.

NARRATOR
Hunter sighs and sinks into the bed beside Toby, spooning his young son. Father and son fall asleep to the sound of crickets and the smell of night-blooming jasmine.

EXT. Rhodes House - YARD — NIGHT

NARRATOR
The windows go dark at the Rhodes place, one by one, the way they always have. The porch light stays on. Just in case.

.EXT. LEROY'S PORCH — NIGHT

DUKE
You ever gonna turn that porch light off? You're feeding every moth in the county.

LEROY
Porch light's staying on. A man's porch light is his own business.

DUKE
Electricity ain't free, you know.

LEROY
(settling deeper into the rocker)
Some things you leave on, Duke. That's just how it's done.

NARRATOR
And away down the county road, at the Rhodes place, somebody agrees with him.

